:Namespace Assert
    ⍝ Assertions for test functions.
    ⍝ Each assertion returns 1 on success, so a dfn test can end with one.
    ⍝ On failure it signals #.Tester.FAIL_EN, which the runner reports as
    ⍝ FAIL rather than ERROR.

    ⎕IO ⎕ML←1

    fail←{
        ⍝ ⍵ ←→ failure message
        ⎕SIGNAL⊂('EN'#.Tester.FAIL_EN)('Message'(,⍕⍵))
    }

    eq←{
        ⍝ ⍺ ←→ expected value
        ⍝ ⍵ ←→ actual value
        ⍺≡⍵:1
        fail'expected ',(show ⍺),' but got ',show ⍵
    }

    ok←{
        ⍝ ⍵ ←→ value that must be exactly 1
        1≡⍵:1
        fail'expected 1 but got ',show ⍵
    }

    raises←{
        ⍝ (fn raises en) arg
        ⍝ ⍺⍺ ←→ function under test
        ⍝ ⍵⍵ ←→ expected error number
        ⍝ ⍵  ←→ argument passed to ⍺⍺
        fn←⍺⍺
        got←{0::⎕DMX.EN ⋄ 0⊣fn ⍵}⍵
        got≡⍵⍵:1
        got≡0:fail'expected error ',(⍕⍵⍵),' but no error was signalled'
        fail'expected error ',(⍕⍵⍵),' but got error ',⍕got
    }

    ⍝ Array notation serialiser. Interactive sessions provide it as
    ⍝ ⎕SE.Dyalog.Array; dyalogscript starts with an empty ⎕SE, so it is
    ⍝ loaded from the installation instead.
    loadArrayNotation←{
        0::⎕FIX'file://',(2 ⎕NQ'.' 'GetEnvironment' 'DYALOG'),'/StartupSession/Dyalog/Array.apln'
        ⎕SE.Dyalog.Array
    }
    ArrayNotation←loadArrayNotation ⍬

    show←{
        ⍝ Single-line array notation, so shape and type differences are
        ⍝ visible: (1⋄) vs 1, ⍬ vs '', '1' vs 1.
        ⍝ The serialiser pads brackets with diamonds ([⋄1 2⋄3 4⋄]); those are
        ⍝ removed outside string literals. Parentheses keep theirs, because
        ⍝ (1⋄) is how a one-item vector is written.
        s←⊃1 ArrayNotation.Serialise ⍵
        '''[^'']*''' '\[⋄' '⋄\]'⎕R(,¨'&[]')⊢s
    }

:EndNamespace
